## 11. Git Hook Integration

### Hooks installed by `init --local`

`.git/hooks/post-checkout`, `post-merge`, `post-commit`, `post-rewrite`.

Each follows this template:

```bash
#!/bin/sh
# mfaw:managed v1
PREV="$(git config --get mfaw.previousPostCheckout 2>/dev/null)"
[ -x "$PREV" ] && "$PREV" "$@"

# Skip during rebase / sparse / temp checkouts
case "$(pwd)" in /tmp/*|/var/folders/*) exit 0 ;; esac

# Background sync — never block git
if command -v mfaw >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  (mfaw sync --quiet >/dev/null 2>&1 &)
fi

exit 0
```

### Hook chaining

If `.git/hooks/post-checkout` already exists, save its path to
`git config mfaw.previousPostCheckout` before overwriting. The new hook
chains to it on every fire.

### Removal (`nuke --local`)

For each hook installed:
1. Read the file.
2. Check first 3 lines for `# mfaw:managed v1`.
3. If present, delete the file. Restore previous from
   `git config mfaw.previousPostCheckout` if set.

### Why per-repo `.git/hooks/`, not `core.hooksPath`

`core.hooksPath` overrides ALL hooks for that repo and breaks any user
tooling that uses git hooks (husky, pre-commit). Per-repo `.git/hooks/`
with chaining is invisible to other tools.

